#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162887} :activity/id #uuid "86e18590-207d-5dd0-a239-bda46bcecd0a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050751 :topic/id #uuid "63937777-cc36-5732-9734-cc239dfdbe45" :topic/math-academy-id 3025} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162887 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163240 :tutorial/content "The **modes of a random variable $X$** with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFor example, the modes of the random variable $X$ with the probability density function shown below are ${\\color{blue}5}$ and ${\\color{blue}9}$ (the values of $x$ that correspond to the global maxima):\n\n![](images/49/49e3f3c63c01ce5c00e9d2ff51bcd03d7516f7866f510ce091071a13a8496210.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "e0f59103-34ab-59f2-b4c8-397a02d782d1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4943 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b08187da-7bd5-55f1-ab34-d07dd9eff9c6" :step/math-academy-id 30822 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162888}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162888 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162001 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161793 :question/id #uuid "21160b89-bb32-5dc5-9a4a-f7589905c913" :question/math-academy-id "e-8241"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6553801-8b7e-5584-940d-a85600b44ec6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048123}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482573}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Using a Graph: One Mode"} :step/id #uuid "a92590f7-aa6f-5fea-a68a-758cda01da2f" :step/math-academy-id 30823 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162889}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162889 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162002 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161794 :question/id #uuid "32f1849f-60e5-58bd-823a-f6bbbdb51153" :question/math-academy-id "e-8242"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "469819e4-0596-5aba-944a-52609afc8af7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496311}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Using a Graph: Multiple Modes"} :step/id #uuid "1a48bd4a-b0a3-502d-aa65-671deff1ee2f" :step/math-academy-id 30824 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162950}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162890 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162003 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161795 :question/id #uuid "e1167d53-7cfb-5ec2-96df-250e226d60b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8243"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12f0497c-3359-5aeb-83d7-930ba3d1ab0d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496243}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically: Multiple Modes"} :step/id #uuid "d5933bd2-e414-5eef-bf2e-16c7bba99353" :step/math-academy-id 30825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162891 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162004 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161796 :question/id #uuid "8be540db-a27c-537c-a804-35ed7cc45168" :question/math-academy-id "e-8244"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e51181c-2074-5d7d-a9b8-d6f6128bc44c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048162}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496269}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically: One Mode"} :step/id #uuid "aecb909d-327f-5105-aed5-aa3bf9d6a261" :step/math-academy-id 30826 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162890}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162950 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163259 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose we are given the random variable $X$ with the probability density function shown below. \n\n![](images/ec/ec48a5d839f3d843fe4738707ec3d067a19db9b009f4bf29673d899b321248a3.svg)\n\nHow can we find the mode of $X$ algebraically? \n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ is $f(x) =2x.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions  of  $f'(x) = 0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x = 0,1.$ So, these are critical points.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =2(0)=0 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf({\\color{blue}1}) =2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is ${\\color{blue}1}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5f6e1736-8d9b-5bc6-8af5-d7618de6d2f8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5172 :tutorial/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically"} :step/id #uuid "45276fe4-2cb4-586a-9f20-43a83aff5f9f" :step/math-academy-id 32476 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162891}}] :activity/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162179}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496335} :activity/id #uuid "afd4e445-adea-544e-9717-93822345549e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050751 :topic/id #uuid "63937777-cc36-5732-9734-cc239dfdbe45" :topic/math-academy-id 3025} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496335 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cd919b6-d9a5-5867-a6da-8315148dae77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495568} {:answer/id #uuid "22e3400e-46e9-5910-8fa1-93c60030b219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495587} {:answer/id #uuid "31890b7f-ed6a-5164-9d49-0ee7fd1e023b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495623} {:answer/id #uuid "7a885735-a293-5a6e-b9f0-e71c16d2d6ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495773} {:answer/id #uuid "b35f84fc-e294-5051-9663-cb93c85382e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495914}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495914} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9ba206c-1c09-5d73-aac2-7ac035ea537b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "58a9cc29-6dde-501b-9df3-e43459e6c342" :question/math-academy-id "q-95331" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $x\\ge 0$ is $f(x)=4{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =8x{e}^{-2x}-8{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}=8{e}^{-2x}x(1-x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n8{e}^{-2x}x(1-x)\\,⟹\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[0,\\infty ),$ so we keep them.\n\n- We've already considered the endpoint $x=0.$\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =4\\cdot {0}^{2}{e}^{-0}=0 \\\\ f(1) & =4\\cdot {1}^{2}{e}^{-2}=4{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(1)=4{e}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $1.$"} :step/id #uuid "06846f48-e9ed-5c06-b8d3-fac94dc4dffb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496381}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496340 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496269 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fdf54c2-2921-5ec5-b47f-e60a64e832fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495620} {:answer/id #uuid "43a25aad-1096-53f8-a1b0-ffe4ce93e7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495665} {:answer/id #uuid "8926741b-753b-5675-b54b-a9f9a86b3ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495808} {:answer/id #uuid "b1502135-3cf5-544d-b180-cf421c12bfbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495909} {:answer/id #uuid "d667db61-2218-508d-88b5-9f484b8424be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495909} :answer-field/id #uuid "e45ad8ad-0b7a-5fe0-93c7-2567d8e280e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496202}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "67c34d9e-6885-5f7c-abc2-3a7d77eaaea5" :question/math-academy-id "q-126319" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{24}x,\\, & 1\\le x\\le 7 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $1\\le x\\le 7$ is $f(x)=\\frac{1}{24}x.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions  of  ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=1,7.$ So, these are critical points.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=1,7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot 1=\\frac{1}{24} \\\\ f(7) & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot 7=\\frac{7}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(7)=\\frac{7}{24}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $7.$"} :step/id #uuid "1fc66cc0-0ba2-5441-b1ec-20054c281e0b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496343 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49d087e9-36d9-5c1d-977a-51607b4bc92e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3,9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495679} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f7066d-8c76-5171-acaa-21fb48763b2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4,7,10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495877} {:answer/id #uuid "a42d220b-ba0b-5a9c-97ee-61a553ddce1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7,10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495878} {:answer/id #uuid "b94bc8ac-78b2-5723-8652-c01c4e2aec68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495922} {:answer/id #uuid "f27b6be5-6904-575d-8c49-1d0b888c6e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4,7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496039}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495877} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8bbfbca-ebe9-54a2-a89e-68cf7149b2de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cb98fa7b-b950-5181-aa33-ef959985aa54" :question/math-academy-id "q-126301" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e5180db140f9567f6863eac3a4bedc498830c2f3e427d04480f61dfa3a64b3ce.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown above. What are the modes of $X?$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that the global maxima of $f(x)$ are at $x=4,7,10.$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $4,7$ and $10.$\n\n![](images/13/13de2513361cca92312190fb75d3c42a508de46efdc753ee6f6257c72e23c3e6.png)"} :step/id #uuid "33f7ee40-34fa-5548-8ce8-002f6236503b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496380}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496380 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fed614a-a35c-5c78-8079-e19a4b70e2a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495621} {:answer/id #uuid "3a042cdc-f2a9-5a30-bd6d-8da210b1ffee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1,3,9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495645} {:answer/id #uuid "40166c8f-84c5-52fa-ada2-77e8a55af142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1,9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495658} {:answer/id #uuid "d74f2c57-186b-515a-aace-7e2faca7126f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1,3,9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495984} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f6e3d5-7b3a-510a-a0b4-233f40962a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3,9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496020}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495984} :answer-field/id #uuid "120d7af0-9d99-54b0-9a46-fee359cefc49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3ee32b00-5c8a-53d2-a621-006bbc7ec2f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-126318" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bcb83928fdc185929add5a26c49289834c70af2b85aa3a856dcecdba73138788.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown above. What are the modes of $X?$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that the global maxima of $f(x)$ are at $x=-1,3,9.$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-1,3$ and $9.$\n\n![](images/6e/6e5f9b96a81ee467251cbe7f1792b3bb8c58bb454e2ec3684173ca798ca0f9d9.png)"} :step/id #uuid "bb6f0075-20ba-517a-b001-600e3aec1da8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496340}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496381 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "562298c6-72ee-5938-8d58-eb91a47a9605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0,3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495706} {:answer/id #uuid "6caa4140-d1ac-55c9-a193-10e41b581be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495750} {:answer/id #uuid "8af47cc7-2ec7-566f-b27b-062280eae539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{2},3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495816} {:answer/id #uuid "dcbb8c12-c911-598d-ab85-055bef131cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495996} {:answer/id #uuid "f65de3f9-aea8-580f-b207-a07101ca1006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2},3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496047}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495750} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b20b945-8756-5419-8b95-3531f4fb01ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3a2c219c-ccba-5ebb-bb19-2884f7df2aa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95376" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}{3\\pi +2}, & 0\\le x\\le 3\\pi  \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 3\\pi $ is $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}{3\\pi +2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3\\pi +2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3\\pi +2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+k\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer.\nAmong these critical points, only three lie within the interval $[0,3\\pi ].$ These are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{2},$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,3\\pi .$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{2},3\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+1}{3\\pi +2}=\\frac{1}{3\\pi +2} \\\\ f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+1}{3\\pi +2}=\\frac{2}{3\\pi +2} \\\\ f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+1}{3\\pi +2}=0 \\\\ f(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})+1}{3\\pi +2}=\\frac{2}{3\\pi +2} \\\\ f(3\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )+1}{3\\pi +2}=\\frac{1}{3\\pi +2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=\\frac{2}{3\\pi +2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "bfd6a877-6320-59fa-87b4-66c1735e4266" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496343}}] :activity/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495505}]}
